  "narrative": "The only due validation response is H12. All H12 estimates are already signed toward the registered stronger-dependency direction, so positive values support the claim and negative values oppose it. The discovery estimate R12 was small and signed negative, -0.0099, with a 95% interval crossing zero. The independent validation R37 was small and signed positive, +0.0137, and its adjusted interval is extremely wide, crossing zero and spanning clinically important values in both directions. Even though outcome_014 has a smaller approximate absolute 10% threshold because its mean is near -0.093, the validation point estimate is not credible given the interval width and the opposite discovery signal. Therefore the validation does not rescue the original claim; it strengthens the view that this predictor-outcome association is unstable and clinically unsupported. H12 remains rejected and closed. More broadly, the accepted set remains empty. The delivered evidence for balanced binary predictors feature_034, feature_002, and feature_060 is consistently small, inconsistent across outcomes, or opposite to the hypothesized stronger-dependency direction. For the stable dependency outcomes with means near -0.25, these estimates are far below the approximate 0.025 absolute threshold. The high-prevalence screens feature_008, feature_047, and feature_023 are also near null or below threshold. Earlier categorical candidates failed cross-outcome corroboration: feature_028 level_003 versus level_002 produced one suggestive outcome_011 result but was near-null, small, or opposite across multiple other outcomes and relied on a small comparator cell; feature_019 failed outcome replication; feature_048 and feature_030 panels were inconsistent or below threshold. These negative results do not prove the absence of true associations, but within the supplied dataset the tested measured predictors do not show scientifically meaningful differences in CRISPR dependency outcomes under the stated 10% clinical-significance rule. The most informative next analysis is to test the remaining active moderate-prevalence binary screens H56 through H63, which have adequate exposed and comparator cell sizes and cover outcome_002, outcome_012, outcome_005, outcome_015, outcome_003, outcome_016, outcome_013, and outcome_011. The outcome_013 categorical screens H45 and H46 should remain active but lower priority. I defer the currently registered interaction claims H29 through H32 and H76 because their underlying main effects are largely null and interaction tests are more multiplicity-prone; they should be revived only if a main-effect candidate emerges or if main-effect screening exhausts. No further independent validation is useful now because no candidate has a threshold-magnitude estimate with a confidence interval credibly excluding clinically unimportant values, or reproducible threshold-exceeding estimates across outcomes. All conclusions are limited to observational associations in this supplied dataset and do not imply causality or generalizability."
